      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <h3>Can I keep using a spreadsheet instead of a CRM?</h3>

      <p>A spreadsheet can work while your contact list and handoffs are simple. Consider a CRM when reminders, shared records or reporting become difficult to maintain reliably. Test the workflow you need before migrating, and compare the time saved with setup, training and ongoing administration.</p>

      <h3>Do small businesses really need a CRM?</h3>

      <p>A small business needs a CRM when its current process cannot reliably track contacts, assign follow-up or show where opportunities stand. A spreadsheet may be enough for a simple workflow. Choose based on missed tasks, team handoffs and reporting needs, rather than a fixed number of leads.</p>

      <h3>What is the difference between a CRM and CRM automation?</h3>

      <p>A CRM holds contact records and shows where opportunities stand. CRM automation adds triggers that create tasks, send approved messages or update records when something changes. Some platforms include both. Before choosing software, list the actions you need automated and confirm that the relevant plan supports them.</p>

